 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Mute and standby",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Pin 5 has two thresholds, both measured from +VS. Above +VS - 2.5 V the amplifier is in standby and the output-stage current generators are off. Between +VS - 2.5 V and +VS - 6 V those generators are on but the amplifier is muted. Below +VS - 6 V it plays. Mute attenuation is 70 dB typ, standby attenuation 110 dB typ, and standby quiescent current 3 mA typ."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power-up and power-down sequence",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "With ±20 V rails, pin 5 starts at VS while the supplies come up, so the part is in standby. It then falls past VS - 2.5 V (mute; quiescent current switches on) and past VS - 6 V to VS - 10 V (play, output follows the input). Going back, pin 5 rises through mute to standby, where quiescent current drops to zero, before the supplies go off. The output is silent in every state but play."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Switching time",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "The capacitor on pin 5 sets the standby and mute time constant: a larger value makes the on and off transitions slower, a smaller value faster."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Gain and stability",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Gain is set by the feedback pair of each channel, 18 kΩ and 560 Ω on the demo board; raising the upper resistor raises gain and raising the lower one lowers it. The closed-loop gain must be 25 dB or more. A 4.7 Ω resistor in series with 0.1 µF on each output keeps the amplifier stable; moving that resistor far from 4.7 Ω either way risks oscillation, as does making the supply bypass capacitors smaller than 1000 µF and 0.1 µF."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bridge operation",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "The two channels can drive one load in bridge. Dissipation and output-stage current limit this mode: with an 8 Ω load keep the supply at ±16 V or less, while a 16 Ω load works across the full supply range. At ±16 V into 8 Ω the bridge gives up to 50 W at 10 % THD. Quiescent current stays as in stereo use, about 80 mA typ at ±16 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Protection",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The output stage is current limited and protected against shorts from output to ground, to +VS and to -VS, in stereo and in bridge use. A thermal shutdown acts at a junction temperature of 145 °C typ. The part has no pop at turn-on or turn-off."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Demo board",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The demo board for the stereo circuit has separate power ground (PGND) and signal ground (SGND) joined by link J1, and terminals for both outputs, both inputs, ±VS and grounds along one edge."
  }
 ],
